---
/** Track validity guide — how GlideComp checks that a submitted tracklog is
 * plausibly the pilot's flight of that task, which checks withhold a track
 * from scoring, and how a scorekeeper overrules the automatic verdict.
 * Sibling of the GAP, open-distance and data-cleaning pages.
 *
 * The thresholds quoted here are the constants in engine track-quality.ts —
 * keep them in step. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BackLink from "../../components/BackLink.astro";
import SpecRef from "../../components/SpecRef.astro";

const S7A_PDF =
  "https://www.fai.org/sites/default/files/civl/documents/sporting_code_s7_a_-_cross_country_2025.pdf";
---

<Base
  title="GlideComp - How Track Validity Checks Work"
  description="How GlideComp checks that a tracklog is really that pilot's flight of that task — which checks withhold a track from scoring, which only flag it, and how a scorekeeper overrules the verdict."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground p-6">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <BackLink href="/scoring" label="Scoring" />
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold flex items-center gap-3">
          <img src="/icon.svg" alt="GlideComp logo" class="w-8 h-8" />
          How Track Validity Checks Work
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">
          Checking that a tracklog is really your flight of this task
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* Table of Contents */}
      <nav class="mb-10 p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Contents
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li><a href="#why" class="hover:underline">Why Tracks Get Checked</a></li>
          <li><a href="#withheld" class="hover:underline">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</a></li>
          <li><a href="#flags" class="hover:underline">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#score" class="hover:underline">What It Does to the Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#override" class="hover:underline">The Scorekeeper Decides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#calibration" class="hover:underline">Where the Thresholds Come From</a></li>
          <li><a href="#disclosure" class="hover:underline">Nothing Happens Silently</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      {/* Why */}
      <section id="why" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Why Tracks Get Checked</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A pilot sometimes uploads the wrong file: a flight from another day, or from another
            site. The mistake is easy to make and difficult to see. The file is valid IGC, it
            opens, and it looks like a flight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The scoring cannot find this mistake. Tasks often run across UTC midnight. GlideComp
            therefore resolves the start gates and the deadlines <em>against the flight it
            scores</em>, and not against a fixed date. A tracklog from another day therefore
            moves the whole task onto its own day, and scores as if nothing is wrong.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">FAI S7A §4.4.2</a>
            makes this the work of the software. The points that verify a flight must have
            "occurred at reasonable times (e.g. on the day in question)".
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Withheld */}
      <section id="withheld" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Both checks ask one question: <strong class="text-foreground">is this file this
            flight?</strong> Both use margins that no honest entry can reach.
          </p>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong day</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              GlideComp compares the fixes against the calendar day of the task, in the time zone
              of the competition, with four more hours at each end. The check fails only when
              <strong class="text-foreground">every</strong> fix is outside that window. An
              overnight recorder, a dawn launch and a flight across local midnight therefore all
              pass. An error of up to one day is flagged, not withheld. A recorder set one day
              wrong is a common fault on a real flight.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong location</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              No fix comes within <strong class="text-foreground">100 km</strong> of a turnpoint.
              A long course does not make this check more strict, because the turnpoints are
              spread along the course. Every pilot who launched starts metres from one of them.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A track that fails either check stays out of the scoring and out of the field
            analysis. In the field analysis, its clock and its air distort the day's statistics
            for all of the other pilots.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Flags */}
      <section id="flags" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            These three flags describe the flying, not the file. Each one also fires on real
            flights, so GlideComp shows them for a person to judge. They
            <strong class="text-foreground">never change a score</strong>.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-muted-foreground">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Never left the take-off cylinder.</strong> This is
              what a pilot looks like who launches and sinks straight back to the ground, and
              GlideComp correctly scores them the minimum distance. It is also what a recorder
              looks like that the pilot left running on launch.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">No sign of flight.</strong> There is no sustained
              climb (1.5 m/s for two minutes) and no sustained glide (90 seconds of descent at
              0.8 m/s, never below 4 m/s over the ground, and approximately straight). This flag
              catches a vario that recorded the drive up to launch. Speed alone cannot catch it,
              because a retrieve vehicle is faster than a paraglider.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Impossible speed.</strong> The ground speed stays
              for a full minute above approximately 119 km/h for paragliders, or 162 km/h for
              hang gliders, with the tailwind included. The one-minute window keeps GPS jitter
              out. This flag looks for something that is not a glider.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Score */}
      <section id="score" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What It Does to the Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            GlideComp scores a pilot who launches and lands short the
            <a href="/scoring/gap#minimum-distance" class="underline hover:text-foreground">minimum distance</a>,
            approximately 5 km. That does not change. A short flight with bad luck still gets it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A withheld tracklog is different, because it is not evidence that the pilot flew this
            task. Those pilots keep their place in the results with
            <strong class="text-foreground">zero points</strong>, and the reasons stay beside
            their name. GlideComp never removes them from the scores.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            No other pilot's score moves. GlideComp leaves the pilot out of the launch-validity
            count (<SpecRef section="9.1" label="S7F §9.1" />), instead of counting them as a
            pilot who flew. A wrong verdict can therefore only cost that one pilot.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Override */}
      <section id="override" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Scorekeeper Decides</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Under
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">S7A §4.4.6</a>,
            the decision to reject a track log belongs to the
            <strong class="text-foreground">organiser</strong>, not to the software. A
            scorekeeper can accept a flagged tracklog in one action. GlideComp then scores it
            normally, its findings stay visible, and the competition's public audit log records
            the decision.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            If GlideComp withheld your track, upload the right file. If the file you uploaded
            <em>is</em> the right one, speak to the scorekeeper.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Calibration */}
      <section id="calibration" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Where the Thresholds Come From</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A wrong zero for a pilot is worse than a bad file that gets through, so the
            thresholds come from real competition data.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            In <strong class="text-foreground">4,762</strong> archived tracks, the usual flight
            passes within 70 m of a turnpoint. The largest distance that an honest track stayed
            from every turnpoint was 45 km. The 100 km threshold is more than twice that
            distance.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The one-day grace has the same origin. In one archived task, three pilots of eighty
            had recorders stamped a day late. All three flew the task and GlideComp scored them
            normally, and one finished fourth, in goal. A rule that disqualified a one-day offset
            gave a zero to a podium flight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            In all <strong class="text-foreground">5,112</strong> tracks that GlideComp holds,
            the checks withhold exactly one: a New Zealand flight submitted to a Victorian task
            ten days later.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Disclosure */}
      <section id="disclosure" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Nothing Happens Silently</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            When a check fires, the pilot's report card names it, shows the numbers behind it,
            and gives the next step. The results table also labels a withheld track, so a zero
            never looks like a fault.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            This is the same rule as
            <a href="/scoring/data-cleaning" class="underline hover:text-foreground">track data cleaning</a>:
            everything the system did on the way to a score must be visible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
